  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 150 AM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nNo major changes in the long term period, please see previous \ndiscussion...\n\nMedium to long range models in reasonable agreement through late \nweek, with a period of quiet weather expected for southeast Wyoming \nand western Nebraska during the middle of the week. Models show \nmoderate southwesterly flow across the area with the polar jet \nstaying north of the Front Range, and the subtropical jet staying \nwell to the south over Texas and northern Mexico. We're kind of in-\nbetween these two separate storm tracks for most of the week with \nmostly quiet weather expected and near or slightly above average \ntemperatures. A pair of potent Pacific shortwave troughs are \nforecast to impact the area late this weekend and early next week, \nwith the second one likely missing southern Wyoming and remaining \nnorth of the area Tuesday. Models show a surface \"cold front\" \nrapidly moving east and clipping the forecast area to the north. \nHowever, all this front will do is shift low to midlevel winds into \nthe west and increase wind speeds on Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight...producing somewhat warmer downslope winds for a bulk of the \narea, including the high plains. Will need to watch for High Wind \ncriteria for the typical wind prone areas of southeast Wyoming since \nmost models show 700mb winds increasing over 45 knots by Tuesday \nmorning and remaining elevated through Tuesday night with a decent \nsurface pressure gradient and some hint of brief mountain wave \nactivity. At this time, in-house wind guidance shows probability of \nwind gusts over 55 MPH around 20% to 45% for Arlington, Bordeaux, \nand even some of the sub wind prone areas along Interstate 25 near \nDouglas. For now, increase wind speeds up to criteria but did not \nincrease wind gusts over 60 MPH quite yet. Event should be pretty \nbrief and not too widespread, and peak mid Tuesday morning. Models \nhave backed off on winds Tuesday night/early Wednesday with a slight \ndownward trend, so kept those winds lower for now. \n\nDownslope winds should result in relatively mild temperatures for \nthis time of the year Tuesday through Thursday, but not quite as \nwarm as it has been prior to this weekend. Kept high temperatures in \nthe upper 50s to upper 60s, warmest of the eastern plains below 5000 \nfeet and coolest temperatures across Carbon and Albany counties. \nPeak temperatures should occur later in the week with highs \napproaching 70 degrees over the western Nebraska panhandle as an \nupper level ridge axis across the southern United States and the \nGulf amplifies ahead of the next Pacific storm system. Model \nsolutions begin to diverge at this time, which is to be expected \nnearly 7 days out, as potential long wave trough attempts to settle \nacross the eastern Pacific Ocean with a potent storm system pushing \ninland and into the Great Basin Region by late next Saturday and \ninto next Sunday. Will have to keep an eye on this storm later in \nthe forecast period as some model solutions and ensembles show a \ndecent amount of colder air along with a subtropical moisture tap \nfrom the central Pacific. This pattern typically favors lee-side low \npressure systems and an active weather pattern for the Front\nRange.",
